Improper charging practices, including overcharging and\nincompatible replacement of batteries and chargers: Low- 4. Battery tampering: Incorrect refurbishing or reassembling\nquality batteries have an increased risk of fire when they are techniques can make used batteries more susceptible to fires.\nleft to recharge unattended overnight.18 If they overheat, the\n5. Prolonged use: Continuous use without proper cooling can\nbatteries can explode, causing a fast-moving fire that is difficult\nincrease the battery’s internal temperature, leading to thermal\nrunaway and fires.\nThermal Runaway Phenomenon 6. Age and degradation: Lithium-ion batteries deteriorate with\ntime, which can increase the risk of overheating. While they have\nThe lithium-ion batteries used in many micromobility devices\na relatively long shelf life compared to other types of batteries,\nare vulnerable to a phenomenon called thermal runaway.19\novercharging or exposure to extreme temperatures can speed up\nThermal runaway is a condition in which damage to one\nthe degradation process within the battery and shorten its shelf\nlithium-ion battery cell can cause the overheating of nearby\nlife. As a general guideline, manufacturers often recommend\ncells. The phenomenon is triggered by battery defects or\nthat lithium-ion batteries be stored in a cool, dry place at a\nissues with chargers, causing an uncontrollable release\npartial state of charge if they are not going to be used for an\nof stored energy, which can result in an explosion or fire.20\nextended period.\nBecause the battery system fails gradually, the fire produces\na lot of heat over time, which can be dangerous, especially 7. Poor maintenance: Improper maintenance of e-bikes and\nin residential settings. Thermal runaway events can also batteries, such as not cleaning terminals or not checking for\nproduce toxic gases which endanger people and property. corrosion or wear on a regular basis, can increase the danger of\nfire because of deteriorating electrical connections.", ""]}, {"row": ["New Jersey Micromobility Guide"]}, {"row": ["How Can Consumers Prevent Lithium-Ion Battery Fires? • Review a device or battery manufacturers’ product\nspecifications, which should clearly state whether their\n1. Buy high-quality batteries: When purchasing a new\nproduct meets industry standards and should provide clear\nmicromobility device, prioritize choosing an e-bike or e-scooter\ndocumentation about the battery’s construction and safety\nthat uses lithium-ion batteries from reliable producers who follow\nfeatures. Look for products that incorporate these battery safety\nrelevant safety regulations. Consumers can rely on a variety of\nfeatures:\nresources to help identify high-quality batteries.\n» Battery Management System (BMS): A strong BMS can\n• Look for positive customer reviews and testimonials on\nmonitor your battery’s voltage, current, temperature, and\nreputable e-commerce platforms, manufacturer websites, or\nstate of charge to avoid overcharging, overexerting, or\nindependent review sites to determine if a product is reliable.\noverheating.21\n• Check whether a battery has an appropriate safety\n» Thermal Management: A battery pack with efficient thermal\ncertification. UL offers battery and e-mobility device safety\nmanagement technologies, such as cooling fans, thermal\ncertifications which manufacturers must undergo rigorous\npads, or heat sinks disperses heat and prevents thermal\ntesting to obtain. The New York City Council’s 2023 law\nrunaway.\non micromobility device and battery safety and the U.S.\nConsumer Product Safety Commission (CPSC) both cite three » Fault Tolerance: To lessen the effects of component\nkey certifications: failures and avert catastrophic fires, higher quality batteries\nincorporate redundancy and fault-tolerant elements into the\n» UL2271 for lithium-ion batteries in light electric vehicles\nbattery system.\n(LEVs)\n• Prioritize products with a robust warranty and accessible\n» UL2849 specifically for the electrical systems of e-bikes\ncustomer support channels to address any potential issues or\nand electrically power-assisted cycles (EPACs)\nconcerns regarding battery performance and safety.\n» UL2272 for the electrical systems of personal e-mobility\n• Research the reputation of e-mobility device and battery\ndevices, including e-scooters, e-skateboards, and\nmanufacturers before buying. Well-established companies tend\nhoverboards\nto prioritize quality and safety in their products.\n• Explore online forums related to micromobility. They can\nprovide information about other consumers’ first-hand\nexperiences, recommendations, and insights on product\nperformance and safety.", ""]}, {"row": ["New Jersey Micromobility Guide"]}, {"row": ["2. Charge safely: Always follow the manufacturer’s instructions\nwhen charging, storing, and maintaining your e-bike battery.22\nOnly use chargers authorized by the manufacturer and avoid\novercharging. Avoid aftermarket or non-matching chargers.\n• Avoid charging batteries in higher risk locations, such as near\nflammable materials, and ensure that batteries are physically\nseparated to prevent thermal runaway.23\n• Avoid charging overnight or leaving charging batteries\nunattended.24 If the micromobility device is not going to be\nused for 12 hours or more, remove the battery from the device.\n• Avoid charging in parking garages or apartment entryways.\n• Do not charge damaged batteries or batteries that have been\nsubmerged in water for any amount of time.\n• Connect chargers directly to wall outlets. Do not use power\nstrips or extension cords.\n• Use outlet timers or set a manual timer to remind yourself to\ndisconnect a device when it is finished charging.25\n• Use fireproof battery bags made of glass fabric woven with\nsteel threads that can contain fires if they occur. 26\n3. Store safely: Devices using lithium-ion batteries should be kept dry\nand cool, away from combustible objects. Do not store e-bikes,\ne-scooters or their batteries in direct sunlight or where they\nmay block the means of egress or fire escape routes. For devices\nAlways follow the manufacturer’s instructions when charging, storing, and\nbeing stored indoors, consider options that offer containment in\nmaintaining your e-bike battery. (NJDOT Bicycle and Pedestrian Resource Center)\nthe event of a thermal runaway event. Seek storage options that\nare fireproofed and well-ventilated.", ""]}, {"row": ["New Jersey Micromobility Guide"]}, {"row": ["4. Inspect regularly: Check micromobility devices and batteries for\nwear and damage on a regular basis. If any problems arise, contact\nthe manufacturer or a specialist with detailed documentation.\nCompanies often replace faulty batteries under warranty, but\ntampering may void a warranty. Servicing and replacing cells should\nnot be done by anyone without specific training on lithium-ion\nbatteries.\n5. Dispose safely: In addition to disposing of old batteries during\nreplacement, damaged batteries should also be disposed of\naccording to local regulations. When lithium-ion batteries are\npartially or totally submerged in water, they become significantly\ncompromised, leading to safety and stability issues. Water exposure,\nespecially salt water, can cause irreversible damage, elevating the\nrisk of dangerous fires that are hard to control and can emit toxic\ngases.27\n• Immediate Action: If a device or its battery is suspected to have\nbeen submerged in water or otherwise damaged, promptly and\ncarefully remove the battery from the device and place it in a\nsafe, outdoor area away from flammable materials.\n• Safe Disposal: Damaged batteries should not be reused or\nrecharged. Instead, they should be disposed of properly at a\nmunicipal household hazardous waste drop-off center. Proper\ndisposal prevents environmental damage and reduces the risk\nof fire.\n• Professional Guidance: Consult a professional, such as the\nmanufacturer or authorized repair center, while making any\ndecisions regarding a potentially damaged lithium-ion battery. After a lithium-ion battery fire is extinguished, firefighters place the battery in a\n55-gallon drum filled with CellBlockEX to prevent reignition. The above photos were\ntaken in a firehouse. (Stephen Dunn)", ""]}, {"row": ["New Jersey Micromobility Guide"]}, {"row": ["When providing a separate charging and storage space, consider the\nHow Can Property Owners and Managers Prevent\nfollowing:\nLithium-Ion Battery Fires?\nUse approved chargers: Use or enforce the use of approved chargers\nProperty owners and managers in large buildings may benefit from\nmade specifically for e-bike and e-scooter batteries. Avoid the use of\nproviding a safe, separate location for micromobility battery charging\nlow-quality or incompatible chargers that may be dangerous.\nand storage. Often the best option is to provide separate charging\nfacilities away from residences where it is possible. Still, properties Designate fireproof storage locations: Construct or retrofit storage\nwith insufficient space to provide optimal separation can designate a facilities, rooms, or physical barriers built in compliance with local\nsafe space outside residences where a fire can be properly contained, construction requirements using fire- and blast-resistant materials.\nreducing the risk of damage and injury to residents in the case\nInstall fire and smoke detection systems: These systems reduce\nof a fire.\nthe likelihood of fires spreading undetected by signaling potential\nproblems and allowing time to take action.\nEnsure proper ventilation: Make sure charging infrastructure is\nNew York City’s “Charge Safe, Ride Safe”\nequipped with proper ventilation if it is provided inside storage\nPilot Program\nspaces.28\nTo improve safety around lithium-ion battery usage in\nInstall self-closing doors: While often required of owners of larger\ne-bikes and e-scooters, New York City Mayor Eric Adams\nbuildings, all property owners can benefit from installing self-closing\nannounced in 2023 “Charge Safe, Ride Safe: New York City’s\ndoors that help prevent the spread of fires once they start\nElectric Micromobility Action Plan.” The plan addresses fire\nrisks by promoting safe usage, raising awareness, pushing\nfor federal regulation, and stepping up enforcement against\nillegal devices. The plan also recognizes the vital role that\nmicromobility devices serve in offering affordable and\nRemember: If a fire does start, leave the building\npractical mobility options for New Yorkers, including many\nimmediately, closing doors behind you to prevent\ndelivery workers. Through pilot initiatives, the creation of\nthe fire from spreading. Once you are safely out\npublic charging infrastructure, and intensive safety education\nof the building and away from the fire, call 911.\ncampaigns, the effort seeks to facilitate the shift to safer\ne-micromobility usage.", ""]}, {"row": ["New Jersey Micromobility Guide"]}, {"row": ["Riding on Trails and Other Off-Road Facilities\nNew Jersey is home to an array of off-road facilities built so pedestrians In national forests and grasslands managed by the National Forest\nand micromobility users can travel, exercise, socialize, and explore Service, conventional bicycles and all classes of e-bikes are allowed\nboth built and natural environments. The design of these facilities can on motorized trails and roads.31 E-bikes are generally not allowed on\nvary greatly from natural surface dirt trails in protected forests to wide non-motorized trails, except where local Forest Service officials have\nboardwalks along the shore. While State law includes references to permitted them. It is important to note that micromobility devices,\nnatural surface trails and bicycle paths, it does not provide definitions including conventional bicycles, are never permitted off-road within\nfor trails, bicycle paths, or shared use paths, meaning there is a lack designated wilderness areas (Brigantine and Great Swamp).32\nof uniform guidance at the statewide level for which modes are\nallowed to use these types of facilities. In many cases, the counties\nPaved Bicycle Paths\nand municipalities that own trails and off-road facilities provide their\nown set of regulations. Title 39 also refers to “bicycle paths” and situations where a\nbicycle is operated on “any path set aside for the exclusive use of\nbicycles.”33 34 However, the vehicle code never defines this term. A\nNatural Surface Trails (Including Bare Earth and\nbicycle path is also not defined in the NJDOT Roadway Design Manual.\nWood Chip)\nAccording to State law, a low-speed electric bicycle or low-speed\nNatural surface trails are intended for use by pedestrians and non-\nelectric scooter may be operated on bicycle paths, though local\nmotorized vehicles. Title 39 defines natural surface trails as those\ngovernments and State agencies are allowed to prohibit these devices\nwhich have “a natural surface tread made by clearing and grading\nthe soil and no surfacing materials have been added.”29 By default,\nconventional bicycles are the only form of micromobility allowed on\nnatural surface trails. However, you should always check the rules\nprovided by the government entity or agency which maintains the\ntrail to confirm that bicycles are allowed.\nAccording to Title 39, low speed e-bikes and e-scooters are prohibited\nfrom natural surface trails.30 However, the law does allow the local\ngovernment entity or State agency with jurisdiction over a trail to\npermit low-speed e-bikes and e-scooters. Since regulations will\nvary from trail to trail, always check local signage and regulations\nbefore riding.\nPaved bicycle path in Camden, NJ. (NJDOT Bicycle and Pedestrian Resource Center)", ""]}, {"row": ["New Jersey Micromobility Guide"]}, {"row": ["on bicycle paths under their jurisdictions. Always refer to signage Typically, signs and/or pavement markings will indicate which types\nposted on or along the path to confirm what the local rule is. Title 39 of micromobility are allowed. However, some jurisdictions have\ndoes not clarify whether motorized bicycles are allowed or prohibited blanket restrictions on the use of bicycles or e-scooters on paths or\non paved bicycle paths. trails within parks. Similarly to Jersey City, Essex County prohibits the\nuse of bicycles on all non-paved roadways within County parks unless\notherwise expressly permitted or specifically designated by signage.36\nShared-Use/Multi-Use Paths\nBecause this space is shared, users operating human-powered or\nA shared-use or multi-use path is one designed to be shared by\nlow-speed electric micromobility have to yield the right-of-way to\npedestrians, bicycles, scooters and other human-powered recreational\npedestrians.37 Instead of posted speed limit signs, shared-use paths\nvehicles. While Title 39 does not provide a statewide definition for\ntypically trust that riders will use their judgement to regulate behavior.\nshared-use paths, there are often local ordinances defining these\nThe City of Hoboken states that bicyclists and e-scooter users must\nfacilities and governing their use. For example, Jersey City defines a\n“travel at a rate of speed which is reasonable and prudent under the\nshared-use path as “any paved pathway in the public right-of-way that\nconditions at the point of operation, taking into account the amount\nsupports multiple recreation and transportation opportunities, such\nand character of pedestrian traffic, grade and width of path, and\nas walking, bicycling, and inline skating, excluding trails and walkways\ncondition of surface.”38\nlocated in City parks.”35\nBicycles and other forms of micromobility yield\nto pedestrians on trails and shared-use paths. All\nusers must yield to horses. (NJDOT Bicycle and\nPedestrian Resource Center)", ""]}, {"row": ["New Jersey Micromobility Guide"]}, {"row": ["Some shared-use paths also host equestrian traffic. As horses can\nbe easily spooked, they always have the right of way over all other\ntrail users. Municipalities may further regulate how users of shared-\nuse paths may act. For example, Jersey City ordinances hold that\noperators of e-scooters must yield to bikes in bicycle lanes and shared\nuse paths.39\nBoardwalks\nThe Jersey Shore is home to many popular boardwalks. Due to the\nseasonal popularity of these facilities, local regulations typically\nestablish rules that vary by the time of year, day of the week, and\ntime of day. For example, Seaside Heights does not limit bicycle use\non boardwalks between November and March. However, between\nApril and October, bicycles are not allowed on the boardwalk during\nweekends after noon. Further restrictions are in place during weekdays\nfrom June 15 to Labor Day.40\nMunicipalities may also place restrictions that differ between busier\nand quieter sections of boardwalks. Atlantic City, for example, does\nnot allow bicycles on the boardwalk between noon to 4:00 p.m. year-\nround, except on a section between Connecticut Avenue to Gardner’s\nBasin where riding is always allowed.41\nRegulations surrounding electric-powered mobility also vary by\nmunicipality. Due to the emerging popularity of e-powered devices,\nit is likely that municipalities will continue to modify their ordinances\nin the coming years. You should always check municipal websites for\nLocal regulations for micromobility on boardwalks typically establish rules that vary guidance on local rules.\nby the time of year, day of the week, and time of day. (NJDOT Bicycle and Pedestrian\nResource Center)", ""]}, {"row": ["New Jersey Micromobility Guide"]}, {"row": ["Securing and Parking Devices\nBicycles and Low-Speed E-Bikes\nChoosing a Lock\nLocking your bicycle or e-bike is critical to ensuring that your device is\nsecure from theft and does not pose an obstacle to other bicyclists or\npedestrians. The two most common types of bicycle locks are U-locks\nand cable locks.\nU-locks are sturdier and harder to break than cable locks, making\nthem the safer option for securing a bicycle. However, U-locks can be\nheavy and usually require a separate key, which could be misplaced.\nU-locks are also less flexible than cable locks, meaning they might not\nfit when parking in a crowded bicycle rack.\nWhen locking your bike, try to find a dedicated bicycle rack instead of locking your\nbicycle to a tree or a fence. (NJDOT Bicycle and Pedestrian Resource Center)\nCable locks, on the other hand, are flexible and usually use a combination\nlock rather than a separate key. For this reason, cable locks are a popular\nchoice for children who are likely to be parking their bicycles on crowded\nschool bicycle racks and don’t want to keep track of a separate set of\nkeys. However, cable locks are less sturdy than U-locks and, with the\nright set of tools, someone can cut through them with ease.\nWith benefits and drawbacks to each style, choosing the right lock is a\nmatter of personal choice. Some people choose to use both a U-lock\nU-locks and cable locks are two options for securing your micromobility device.\nand a cable lock at the same time.\n(NJDOT Bicycle and Pedestrian Resource Center)", ""]}, {"row": ["New Jersey Micromobility Guide"]}, {"row": ["Locking Your Bike\nRegardless of which lock you purchase, it’s important to\nfollow proper locking techniques. To do this, loop your lock\nthrough the frame of the bicycle and at least one wheel\n(preferably the rear wheel) before securing the lock to the\nrack. Make sure to bring any loose items, such as helmets\nor water bottles, with you when you leave. These might get\nstolen if you leave them unattended. Also, be careful not\nto accidentally loop your lock through an adjacent bicycle\nor lock. See the images to the right for guidance on proper\nand improper locking techniques.\nWhen you reach your destination, try to find a dedicated\nbicycle rack instead of locking your bicycle to a tree or a\nfence. Whenever possible, leave your bicycle in a highly\nvisible area where people are less likely to tamper with it.\nIf your e-bike has a removable battery, consider taking it\nwith you to avoid it being stolen.42 Sheltered parking is also\npreferred to keep your bicycle dry in the event of rain. In\nNew Jersey, bicycles and low-speed (Class 1 and 2) e-bikes\nare allowed to be parked on sidewalks as long as they are\nnot blocking pedestrian access, though dedicated areas\nfor bicycle parking are a better option when provided.\nTechniques for locking a bike using cable locks and U-locks. (The School Bike Parking\nGuide prepared by the NJDOT Safe Routes to School Resource Center at Rutgers University)", ""]}, {"row": ["New Jersey Micromobility Guide"]}, {"row": ["E-Scooters\nSome e-scooters come with built-in locking mechanisms or security\nmeasures that enable the user to turn the device on or off remotely\nwith a key fob or app.43 Even so, a physical lock can provide an\nadditional level of security when parking your device.\nHigh-quality U-locks offer the greatest amount of protection for\ne-scooters because they are difficult to cut through. At the same time,\nU-locks can be heavy and there may be cases when folding locks are a\nmore convenient option. Because they are lightweight, folding locks\ncan be more easily carried in a backpack or mounted to an e-scooter’s\nstem. However, folding locks contain more weak points than U-locks\nand are therefore a somewhat less secure mechanism for locking an\ne-scooter.\nWhen locking your e-scooter, it is important to thread the lock\nthrough a non-removable part of the device. The best locations on an\ne-scooter for locking are:44\n• Around the stem\n• Through the folding mechanism\n• Through a carrying handle that is integrated into the frame\nPoor locations for locking include:\n• Through removable wheels\n• Around the handlebars\n• Through fenders or bolted-on accessories\nWhen locking your e-scooter, it is important to thread the lock through\na non-removable part of the device.\nAs with a bicycle or e-bike, it is safest to lock your e-scooter to a fixed\nobject. Certain types of bicycle racks are better suited for locking\ne-scooters than others, particularly those which allow you to lock\nWhen locking an e-scooter, it is important to thread the lock through a non-removable\nyour e-scooter around the stem or other secure part of the frame.\npart of the device. (riderguide.com)", ""]}, {"row": ["New Jersey Micromobility Guide"]}, {"row": ["Some locations may offer racks that are designed specifically for While e-scooters are generally allowed to be locked to any bicycle\ne-scooters and allow you to lock the scooter’s stem securely to a fixed rack, this is not always clear. In some cases, the inclusion of signage\nstructure.45 While currently less common than bicycle racks, e-scooter- at bicycle parking facilities that specifically mentions e-scooters can\nspecific parking will likely become more prevalent as the popularity help clarify where is best to park these devices.\nof e-scooters continues to increase. As traditional bicycles, e-bikes,\nAs with bicycles and low-speed e-bikes, e-scooters are allowed to\nand e-scooters compete for limited parking, municipalities, counties,\nbe parked on sidewalks as long as they are not blocking pedestrian\nand others who provide micromobility parking should consider\naccess. If you are parking your e-scooter without locking it to another\ninstalling racks designed specifically for e-scooters to accommodate\nstructure, seek out locations near bicycle racks where your device will\nthe growing use of these devices.\nnot block walkways or building entrances.46\nSecure parking designed specifically for scooters. (gogreenspoke.com)", ""]}, {"row": ["New Jersey Micromobility Guide"]}, {"row": ["Traveling with Micromobility on Transit\nMicromobility devices allow people to go further distances without\na car, and for many, these devices are the solution for getting to or\nfrom public transit facilities, such as train stations, bus stops, and light\nrail stations. Some transit agencies allow you to bring micromobility\ndevices with you on the train, bus, or ferry; however, the rules vary\nconsiderably depending on the device, agency, day of the week,\nand even time of day. This section describes best practices for\nmicromobility users to travel to, from, and on transit.\nGeneral Rule #1: If you can, lock and leave it!\nTaking your device with you may not always be the best choice. If your\nfinal destination is within a short walk from the end of your transit trip,\nit may be easier to park and lock your device before getting on the\nTransit agency rules about bringing micromobility on board ules vary considerably\nbus, train, or ferry. If you feel comfortable leaving your device locked\ndepending on the device, agency, day of the week, and even time of day.\nat or near your origin station or bus stop, it can be more convenient\n(NJDOT Bicycle and Pedestrian Resource Center)\nto not have to travel with the extra responsibilities of properly stowing\nyour device or the added risk that there will not be available space on\nyour route.\nFor more information on how best to lock your device, see the best devices vary widely. As such, it is important to check an agency’s\npractices section on Securing and Parking Devices� rules before beginning your trip.\nFor example, many NJ TRANSIT train lines in North Jersey do not allow\nGeneral Rule #2: Know before you go!\nnon-folding micromobility devices during peak times. Additionally,\nIn New Jersey, there are a lot of different transit agencies and private PATH trains, which serve Newark, Harrison, Hoboken, Jersey City, and\ncompanies that serve communities across the state, with NJ TRANSIT New York City, do not allow any electric micromobility devices at any\nbeing the largest transit agency in the state. Between the different time, and non-folding bikes are also not allowed during peak times.\nservices offered by NJ TRANSIT and the many other agencies and Peak times vary by agency and are subject to change based on the\ncompanies, the rules and regulations surrounding micromobility agency’s judgment.", ""]}, {"row": ["New Jersey Micromobility Guide"]}, {"row": ["You should be prepared to stow your vehicle appropriately. On RIVER\nLine trains in South Jersey, all trains come with bike racks. Other trains\nin the state do not have dedicated racks but allow certain devices to\nbe brought on board and stowed in wheelchair-accessible spaces on\na first-come first-served basis. On trains where non-folding devices\nare permitted, single-level railcars can hold up to two devices per car,\nand multilevel railcars can hold up to eight devices per car based on\ncrowding and the accessibility needs of other riders. You should stay\nwith your device if it comes onboard with you in the passenger area.\nNJ TRANSIT buses allow some folding micromobility devices on board;\nhowever, non-folding devices must be stored away from passengers.\nSome bus lines running low-floor (city-style) buses are equipped on\nthe front with bicycle racks, which can fit low-speed electric bicycles\nand conventional bicycles. Coach-style commuter buses run by NJ\nTRANSIT allow some non-folding devices to be stored in the under-\nfloor compartment. As always, space is limited and offered on a first-\ncome first-served basis.\nAmtrak trains require a reservation with a $20 fee per bicycle in\naddition to the cost of the ticket for the passenger. Bicycle space is\nlimited and there are restrictions on the dimensions of bicycles that\ncan fit based on the racks available on a given train. Check Amtrak’s You should always be prepared to stow your device appropriately. (NJDOT Bicycle\nwebsite for more details on the types of racks and how to bring your and Pedestrian Resource Center)\ndevice on their trains.\nFerries operated by NY Waterway allow traditional bicycles, folding\nbicycles, e-scooters, and Class 1, 2, and 3 e-bikes provided they are For more detailed information about which types of devices\nnot gas-powered. Motorcycles, gas-powered scooters, Vespa-style are allowed when and where, refer to the Regulations by\nmopeds, electric skateboards, and segways are prohibited. Device Type section.", ""]}, {"row": ["New Jersey Micromobility Guide"]}, {"row": ["General Rule #3: No gas motors!\nWithout exception, transit agencies prohibit any gas-powered engines\nin stations, on platforms, or on buses, trains, or ferries. Gas vehicles\nemit fumes, sometimes even when the engines are not running. Gas\nengines are also at risk of spilling or leaking oil or gasoline while\naboard a vehicle, which is a fire hazard and adds the risk of toxic fumes,\nunpleasant smells, and oil slicks on vehicles. For these reasons, you\nshould never take a gas-powered device on transit.\nGeneral Rule #4: All that folds is gold!\nMany transit agencies are more permissive of folding micromobility\ndevices. This is often because they require less space and are easier\nto bring on board. Folding devices are more often treated like a carry-\non item, because they can fit closer to you and away from the aisle. If\nyou are purchasing a new micromobility device and expect to take it Being a courteous rider can help people get accustomed to seeing micromobility on\non transit where permitted—especially during rush hour—you should their trip. (NJDOT Bicycle and Pedestrian Resource Center)\nconsider a device that folds.\nGeneral Rule #5: Courtesy can take you a long way!\nbest not only because it reduces the risk of a fall or crash with another\nPublic transit is a communal experience, and everyone is entitled person, but also because it is courteous to others around you.\nto their reasonable use of the services provided; however, it is\nIf trains and buses become crowded over the course of your trip,\nvery important to be conscientious of the comfort of your fellow\nyou should be cognizant of taking up any unnecessary space. While\npassengers. Bulky micromobility devices are often not permitted on\nconductors and drivers should be familiar with the rules regarding what\nbusy trains. Even so, if you are permitted to take your device on a\nis and is not allowed, everyday riders may make different judgments.\ntrain, bus, or ferry, follow the rules and be sure there is room for other\nAs such, it is important to remember that as a micromobility user, your\npassengers to move around you.\nbehavior reflects on those of all micromobility users. Being a courteous\nIt is against the rules to ride any device in stations, on platforms, or rider can help people get accustomed to seeing micromobility on\naboard trains and buses. Space is limited in transit facilities and on their trip and, as a consequence, they may even consider trying out\nvehicles. Walking or carrying your device through transit facilities is and e-bike or e-scooter for themselves.", ""]}, {"row": ["New Jersey Micromobility Guide"]}, {"row": ["Endnotes 13 Pedestrian and Bicycle Information Center. Bike Share. https://www.\npedbikeinfo.org/topics/bikeshare.cfm\n1 New Jersey NJTR-1 Crash Report Manual (https://www.nj.gov/ 14 National Association of City Transportation Officials.\ntransportation/refdata/accident/pdf/NJTR-1CrashReportManual2023. https://nacto.org/wp-content/uploads/2023/11/NACTO_\npdf) sharedmicromobilitysnapshot_correctedNov3-2023-1.pdf\n2 Lombardi, L. R., Pfeiffer, M. R., Metzger, K. B., Myers, R. K., & Curry, A. E. 15 Shared-Use Mobility Center. NewarkGo Pilot Launches for Dockless\n(2022). Improving identification of crash injuries: Statewide integration Bikeshare and E-Scooters. https://learn.sharedusemobilitycenter.org/\nof hospital discharge and crash report data. Traffic Injury Prevention, overview/newarkgo-pilot-launches-for-dockless-bikeshare-and-e-\n23(sup1), S130-S136. https://doi.org/10.1080/15389588.2022.2083